  "question": "If $x^2 + y^2 = 1$, find $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(\\sin^{-1} y)$ for $0 < x < 1$.",
  "answeroption": "C",
  "explanation": "Differentiate $x^2 + y^2 = 1$:<br />$2x + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$<br /><br />$\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{x}{y}$<br /><br />$\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin^{-1} y) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-y^2}} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}$<br /><br />Since $1-y^2 = x^2$, $\\sqrt{1-y^2} = x$<br /><br />$= \\frac{1}{x} \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{y}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{y}$",
